**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier ended without a verdict within 4 turns

Any authenticated caller can set `body.accountId` and read that account. The handler uses `body.accountId ?? caller.accountId` with no check that the caller is support staff or owns the target id, so this is direct object reference on account data.

Failure mode: `POST/GET` with `{"accountId":"victim-id"}` returns the victim row as JSON for a normal user session.

Gate explicit lookup on a staff role/claim from `auth(req)`, return 403 when a non-staff caller supplies a different id, and keep self-lookup as the only unprivileged path. Example shape:

```typescript
const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
const id: string = body.accountId ?? caller.accountId;
if (body.accountId && body.accountId !== caller.accountId && !caller.isSupportStaff) {
  return new Response("Forbidden", { status: 403 });
}
const account = await db.accounts.findUnique({ where: { id } });
```

(Use whatever staff signal `auth` actually exposes; the important part is denying cross-account reads by default.)
